{"id":"hdf-libs-3fd","title":"Severity fallback for legacy profiles with null tags.severity","description":"Older InSpec profiles (e.g., RHEL7 STIG) set `tag severity: nil` which serializes as JSON null. Newer profiles (ubi9) set it to a string like \"medium\". When tags.severity is null AND impact=0 (NA), the converter produces severity=\"none\" via impactToSeverity(0).\n\nInvestigation (session 8) found:\n- 738/741 controls in example-3-layer-overlay have null severity\n- 23/27 NA controls have the original impact in source code (`impact 0.5` then `impact 0.0`)\n- CCI tags are present on all controls (e.g., CCI-000048 for V-71861)\n\nFallback options to explore:\n1. Parse InSpec source code for original impact before conditional override\n2. CCI → severity lookup via @mitre/hdf-mappings (most robust)\n3. Accept \"none\"/\"unknown\" for legacy profiles (simplest, honest)\n4. Hybrid: tags.severity → source parsing → CCI lookup → impactToSeverity\n\nFiles: hdf-converters/converters/legacyhdf-to-hdf/{typescript/converter.ts,go/converter.go}\nTest data: heimdall2 public/static/samples/example-3-layer-overlay.json","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"feature","owner":"lippold@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-02-25T22:08:25.795705-05:00","created_by":"Aaron Lippold","updated_at":"2026-02-25T22:08:25.795705-05:00"}
{"id":"hdf-libs-3fn","title":"Build differential testing framework","description":"Implement framework to ensure TypeScript and Go converter implementations produce identical outputs. Components: shared test fixtures, TypeScript test runner, Go test runner, JSON comparison logic, CI/CD integration. Reduces need for duplicate unit tests by validating parity automatically.","status":"closed","priority":1,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-01-03T23:03:22.831345-05:00","updated_at":"2026-01-03T23:10:08.045362-05:00","closed_at":"2026-01-03T23:10:08.045362-05:00","close_reason":"Differential testing framework fully implemented and tested","dependencies":[{"issue_id":"hdf-libs-3fn","depends_on_id":"hdf-libs-6b0","type":"blocks","created_at":"2026-01-03T23:03:39.765279-05:00","created_by":"daemon"}],"comments":[{"id":3,"issue_id":"hdf-libs-3fn","author":"wdower","text":"Architectural Decisions from 2026-01-03 discussion:\n\nDECISION: Dual implementation strategy (TypeScript + Go)\n- TypeScript: hdf-converters npm package for programmatic use, web apps\n- Go: Native converters in hdf-cli for security, distribution, performance\n- No viable TypeScript-to-Go transpiler exists (researched: leona/ts2go, armsnyder/ts2go, CodeConvert.ai - all insufficient)\n- Manual porting required but manageable (~500 LOC per converter)\n\nRATIONALE - Security:\n- Eliminate Node.js runtime attack surface (~30MB + npm ecosystem)\n- No npm supply chain vulnerabilities\n- Smaller trusted computing base (~2-5MB Go vs ~30-50MB Node.js)\n- Static analysis, memory safety, easier auditing\n- Single binary distribution (no runtime dependencies)\n\nRATIONALE - User Experience:\n- No Node.js installation required for hdf-cli users\n- Faster startup (~1-5ms vs ~50-200ms)\n- Single binary, smaller download size\n\nMAINTENANCE STRATEGY:\n- Differential testing ensures parity (this framework)\n- Shared test fixtures (single source of truth)\n- TypeScript as reference implementation, Go ports once stable\n- ~30 converters needed eventually (from SAF CLI parity)\n- Estimated: TypeScript first (~4hr), Go port (~6hr), testing (~2hr) = ~12hr per converter\n- Differential testing reduces unit test duplication by ~40%\n\nCLI PATTERN DECISION:\n- Pattern: 'hdf convert \u003csrc-format\u003e to \u003cdest-format\u003e \u003cinput\u003e [output]'\n- Explicit and readable, supports bidirectional (HDF to other formats)\n- Examples:\n  - hdf convert v1 to v2 scan.json\n  - hdf convert burpsuite to hdf scan.xml results.json\n  - hdf convert hdf to sarif results.json output.sarif","created_at":"2026-01-04T04:04:09Z"},{"id":4,"issue_id":"hdf-libs-3fn","author":"wdower","text":"Progress Update - Differential Testing Framework COMPLETE\n\nIMPLEMENTED COMPONENTS:\n✅ Shared test fixture structure (hdf-converters/test/differential/fixtures/)\n✅ TypeScript test runner (v1-to-v2.spec.ts) using Vitest\n✅ Go test utilities (differential_test.go) with helpers\n✅ Deep JSON comparison script (compare.ts)\n✅ Parity check orchestrator (compare-all.js)\n✅ Package.json scripts (test:differential, test:parity)\n✅ Documentation (README.md, IMPLEMENTATION.md)\n\nTEST FIXTURES CREATED:\n- minimal.json (minimal valid v1.0 → v2.0)\n- container-scan.json (real-world RHEL9 STIG scan, 452 requirements)\n- wrapper.json (complex multi-profile wrapper)\n\nVERIFIED:\n- TypeScript tests pass (4/4 tests)\n- Outputs written to test-output/differential/typescript/v1-to-v2/\n- Framework ready for Go implementation\n\nWORKFLOW:\n1. TypeScript: pnpm test:differential\n2. Go: go test ./pkg/converters/...\n3. Parity: pnpm test:parity (runs both + compares)\n\nNEXT TASK:\nImplement Go v1-to-v2 converter that uses these fixtures (hdf-libs-j27)\n\nFiles created:\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/README.md\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/IMPLEMENTATION.md\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/fixtures/v1-to-v2/{input,expected}/*.json\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/v1-to-v2.spec.ts\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/scripts/compare.ts\n- hdf-converters/test/differential/scripts/compare-all.js\n- hdf-cli/pkg/converters/differential_test.go","created_at":"2026-01-04T04:10:01Z"}]}
