{"id":"hdf-libs-oi8","title":"Audit converters for format-level commonality","description":"## Architecture Decision: Format-Level Routing\n\n### Problem\nMany security tools output multiple formats (native JSON, SARIF, SonarQube Generic, etc.). Users shouldn't need to know which converter to use — `hdf convert gosec to hdf` should work regardless of whether the input is gosec native JSON or gosec SARIF output.\n\n### Decision: Strategy B — Rich shared format converters + tool-level format detection\n\nTool converters (e.g. gosec-to-hdf) become thin routing layers:\n1. Detect what format the input is in (native JSON? SARIF? JUnit XML?)\n2. If shared format → delegate to shared format converter\n3. If native → use tool-specific parsing\n4. Optionally apply tool-specific enrichment after shared conversion\n\n### Shared Format Converters Needed\n- **SARIF** (covers: gosec, snyk, zap, jfrog-xray, semgrep, CodeQL, trivy, checkov, ESLint, fortify, veracode)\n- **JUnit XML** (covers: test frameworks, CI/CD outputs)\n- **CycloneDX** (covers: SBOM tools — trivy, syft, snyk, cdxgen)\n- **XCCDF** (covers: OpenSCAP, DISA STIGs)\n\n### SARIF Converter Gaps (current → needed)\nCurrent converter is minimal. Must add:\n- `rules[]` definitions (name, description, helpUri)\n- `rules[].relationships` → CWE taxonomy extraction\n- `tool.driver.supportedTaxonomies` → CWE/OWASP awareness\n- `result.suppressions[]` → notReviewed status with justification\n- `rules[].properties` → confidence, tags, custom metadata\n- `rules[].defaultConfiguration.level` → severity when result.level is absent\n- Proper severity granularity from rule metadata\n\n### Impact on Converter Count\n- ~14 proprietary-only tools still need dedicated converters\n- ~5-8 SARIF-capable tools can delegate to enriched SARIF converter\n- 3 standard formats (JUnit, CycloneDX, XCCDF) get dedicated shared converters\n- Net: fewer converters to build and maintain\n\n### Format Detection\nUse fingerprinting (check for `$schema`+`version`+`runs` for SARIF, `\u003ctestsuites\u003e` for JUnit, `bomFormat` for CycloneDX, etc.) rather than requiring the user to specify format.\n\n---\n## Progress\n- [x] SARIF converter enriched with full spec support (2026-02-21): rules[], relationships, taxonomies, suppressions, kind mapping, fixes, codeFlows, snippets, fingerprints, level fallback chain\n- [ ] JUnit XML shared format converter\n- [ ] CycloneDX shared format converter\n- [ ] XCCDF shared format converter","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","owner":"will@dower.dev","created_at":"2026-02-20T20:56:47Z","created_by":"Will","updated_at":"2026-02-22T19:22:55Z","closed_at":"2026-02-21T14:58:33Z","close_reason":"SARIF converter enriched with full 2.1.0 spec support. Remaining shared format converters (JUnit, CycloneDX, XCCDF) will be built as standalone tasks before individual tool converters."}
